1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,760
 Yeah, good morning and welcome to this program on Hands-on Approach to Artificial Intelligence

2
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:11,840
 for Real World Applications.

3
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:18,760
 So, in today's one hour lecture, I am going to introduce what is artificial intelligence.

4
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:25,360
 The details of this techniques will be covered in subsequent lectures by other teachers.

5
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:32,840
 So, just to introduce what is artificial intelligence before that let us see how our natural intelligence

6
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:34,960
 actually works.

7
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:40,160
 So, I start with these two pictures.

8
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:43,640
 The question is do you recognize these pictures?

9
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:49,400
 Now, whether you recognize or not that is not that important, what is important is how

10
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,320
 do you recognize?

11
00:00:51,320 --> 00:01:00,240
 Obviously, I believe that you know the picture on the left is cave painting from Ajanta

12
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:06,480
 caves and picture on the right is our parliament building, old parliament building I will say

13
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:08,200
 in New Delhi.

14
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,960
 Now, how do you recognize?

15
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:18,280
 Naturally, you must have seen these pictures in your history book during your school days

16
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:23,340
 or maybe somebody has shown you these pictures a number of times, possibly some of you have

17
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:29,080
 visited these places, gone to the Ajanta caves and seen these paintings yourselves.

18
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:36,680
 So, when you look or when you see such pictures, what you do is you create a description of

19
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:39,160
 these pictures some form of description.

20
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:45,160
 Why I am saying some form of description is that even if the same objects or same pictures

21
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:51,440
 are shown to you in a distorted fashion, in the sense right now the pictures which are

22
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:59,280
 shown are in color, so if instead of color just black and white version of the same pictures

23
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:04,760
 are shown to you or the pictures are slightly distorted, it is scaled up, scaled down, rotated

24
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:06,880
 even then you will be able to understand.

25
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:16,640
 So, that clearly says that what you have understood is not the picture itself or point by point

26
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:23,520
 content of the picture, but some description you have made of this picture and these picture

27
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:30,080
 these descriptions are stored in your brain in some form of a model.

28
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:37,320
 So, next time when similar such pictures are shown to you, you try to extract similar such

29
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:43,680
 descriptions and then try to associate those new pictures to one of the pictures which

30
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:49,000
 are stored in your brain based on some sort of similarity or dissimilarity.

31
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,120
 So, that is how our natural intelligence works and naturally when you understand something

32
00:02:56,120 --> 00:03:01,840
 or when you form a model this model does not is not formed in a single instance.

33
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:07,920
 Say for example, in your school days when you have started learning the alphabets A,

34
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:14,680
 B, C, D and all, so your parents or your school teachers must have told you A for apple, B

35
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:21,000
 for ball and that has been told a number of times, it is not only once and after hearing

36
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,880
 that after looking at those characters a number of times you have been able to remember it

37
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:33,560
 or you have been able to create a model of that which has been stored in the brain.

38
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:38,920
 So, when you talk about machine learning or artificial intelligence, you find that at

39
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:45,160
 the input we have the signal which are the raw signal, this signal can be an image, this

40
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:49,520
 signal can be some time domain signal something like a speech and all that.

41
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:55,080
 So, when I talk about image, it is the visual recognition that comes into picture and when

42
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:59,800
 it is a time domain signal like some music or speech, it is our auditory system that

43
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,480
 comes into picture.

44
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:06,760
 So, we should be able to recognize whether you are watching a dog, whether you are watching

45
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:09,440
 a bird, what type of bird it is.

46
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:15,040
 Similarly, when it comes to your auditory signal or speech, you should be able to recognize

47
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:22,120
 whether you are listening to say Hemant Kumar or you are listening to Latha Mangaskar and

48
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:23,960
 you can easily identify that.

49
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:26,200
 So, how does it actually work?

50
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:33,200
 So, given an input signal, the first thing as I said that we create a description, some

51
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:34,200
 sort of description.

52
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:39,480
 Similarly, come to coming to machine learning for given signals, we try to find out some

53
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:46,320
 sort of features, not a single one, multiple number of features from those signals and those

54
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:49,240
 features are put in the form of a feature vector.

55
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:54,440
 So, every feature vector represents a particular signal.

56
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:59,120
 Now, how this feature vectors are important?

57
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:04,880
 The moment you convert or you translate a signal from the raw domain say for example,

58
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:11,080
 from image to a vector, the moment I put it in the form of a vector, my recognition task

59
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:13,040
 becomes very easy.

60
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:21,760
 Let us say for example, we have a vector of say three-dimensional, you just extract three

61
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:27,160
 different properties or three different features from the given signal and you have a three-dimensional

62
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:28,160
 feature vector.

63
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:33,520
 Now, once I have this three-dimensional feature vector, then this feature vector represents

64
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:36,240
 a point in our three-dimensional space.

65
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:42,600
 So, if I have two different signals or if I have two instances of the same signal, it

66
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:48,080
 is quite likely that the feature vectors corresponding to those two different signals will be very

67
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:53,720
 similar or they will be very close in the three-dimensional feature space whereas, if

68
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:59,640
 I take two signals which are very wide say for example, I have a picture of a bird and

69
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:03,640
 I have another picture which is the picture of a car.

70
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:08,560
 So, again those two three-dimensional feature vectors that you create one out of bird and

71
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:14,260
 one out of car, these two feature vectors will be very different and in my three-dimensional

72
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:18,640
 feature space, the distance between these two points will be very large.

73
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:25,160
 So, I can just find out whether two signals are similar or they are dissimilar depending

74
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:28,720
 upon what is the distance between the corresponding feature vectors.

75
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:34,760
 So, once I have this feature vectors, then I can put, I can fit those feature vectors

76
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:40,920
 into a classifier or the classifier will classify other or recognize the signal to be whether

77
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:44,320
 it is a bird, whether it is a car, whether it is a dog and all that.

78
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:51,200
 So, I can have different types of classifiers, some are Bayesian classifiers, SVM, neural

79
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:53,080
 network and all that.

80
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:57,800
 Out of that I will put more emphasis on neural network because all your subsequent lectures

81
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:01,920
 are mostly going to be based on neural network approach.

82
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:07,880
 So, let us see when I say descriptors, what are different types of descriptors we have.

83
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:16,040
 So, here I have shown few images, you find that on the top I have the silhouette or just

84
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:22,520
 the boundary of two different animals and you can easily recognize that the left one

85
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:28,880
 is the boundary of a horse and the right one is the boundary of a zebra.

86
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:34,680
 However, given only this boundary information, if I want to compute what is the difference

87
00:07:34,680 --> 00:07:40,240
 between these two boundaries, the difference is very very small or they are very very similar.

88
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:46,640
 So, maybe based on just this, I will come to a bit later that how from these boundaries

89
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:54,360
 we can extract different information which can be useful for differentiating between

90
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:58,120
 these two animals or different types of animals.

91
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:02,320
 But as I said, as I was saying that based on just this boundary information, it is very

92
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:06,200
 difficult to differentiate between a horse and a zebra.

93
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:10,920
 So, in addition to boundary information, so with this boundary information it is quite

94
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:15,280
 possible that I will be able to differentiate between a horse or a zebra that is a class

95
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:20,680
 of horse and zebra and class of birds, but differentiation between a horse and zebra

96
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:22,920
 becomes very very difficult.

97
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:30,320
 So, in addition to this boundary information, we need some more information and you remember

98
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:36,840
 that in case of a zebra, if you look at the body of a zebra, mostly you have black and

99
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:41,800
 white stripes whereas in case of horse, you do not have black and white stripes.

100
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:47,160
 So, along with the boundary information you use some more information which is the region

101
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:53,840
 information and these two informations put together gives you a feature vector which

102
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:58,080
 can be used for differentiation between a horse and a zebra.

103
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:04,880
 Now, when you come to the region property or the region descriptors, we can have intensity

104
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:13,000
 profile as has been shown on the image on the right hand side, you can have color as shown

105
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:19,680
 on the left bottom image or you can also have texture as shown in the right bottom image.

106
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:26,400
 So, these are different types of informations that can be extracted from the same image and

107
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:31,240
 when you talk about this informations, obviously as we are talking about feature vector, so

108
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:38,900
 these informations are to be quantified or I should able to compute some new numerical

109
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:42,560
 values representing these region properties.

110
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:47,560
 So, I will come to that a bit later, let us see what how this boundary descriptor can

111
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:48,560
 be generated.

112
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:55,840
 So, you find that I have given a closed curved boundary and what I can do is, I can find

113
00:09:55,840 --> 00:10:00,400
 out or I can represent this boundary by a polygon.

114
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:06,040
 There are different algorithms, how you can get a polygonal approximation of a curved

115
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:10,600
 boundary I am not going into that, but for the time being let us take it for granted

116
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:13,040
 that given a curved boundary I can have a polygon.

117
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:17,280
 So, what I have is a polygon something like this.

118
00:10:17,280 --> 00:10:24,240
 Once I have such a polygon for every such polygon, obviously I have a number of internal

119
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:30,800
 angles, so let us put this internal angles as alpha 1, alpha 2, alpha 3, alpha 4 and

120
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:31,800
 so on.

121
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:36,160
 Now, these angles can be scanned either in the clockwise direction or in anticlockwise

122
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:45,240
 direction I can use any of them, but I have to keep in mind that if I use clockwise convention

123
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:48,440
 I always have to use clockwise rotation.

124
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:54,280
 If I use anticlockwise I always have to use anticlockwise, so if you scan these images

125
00:10:54,280 --> 00:11:01,280
 either in clockwise or in anticlockwise fashion, then you have you find that I have a sequence

126
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:07,240
 of internal angles say alpha 1, alpha 2, alpha 3 and for 4 and so on.

127
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:11,720
 These internal angles of the sequence now I can take this as a time series, so there

128
00:11:11,720 --> 00:11:18,360
 is no time involved in it, but it is a sequence of some angle values.

129
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:25,240
 In this sequence I can have an autoregressive model over this sequence.

130
00:11:25,240 --> 00:11:32,240
 So, in this autoregressive model what I do is I give an any angle alpha i, ith angle

131
00:11:32,240 --> 00:11:39,800
 I can represent this as a linear combination of p number of previous angles, so the expression

132
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:41,080
 comes something like this.

133
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:47,560
 So, alpha i is equal to beta k alpha i minus k where k varies from 1 to p, so this is a

134
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:49,800
 pth order autoregression model.

135
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:57,040
 So, you find that there are p number of coefficients beta k which are autoregression coefficients

136
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:03,600
 and I will get if there are say n number of internal angles or if the number of vertices

137
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:07,800
 of the polygon are n, I will have n number of such equations.

138
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:13,400
 And you solve all these equations simultaneously and you get the values of your coefficients

139
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:19,560
 beta 1 to beta p and these coefficients this set of coefficients will be different for

140
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:21,200
 different types of polygons.

141
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:28,520
 So, I can represent or I can use these coefficients as some sort of description or approximate

142
00:12:28,520 --> 00:12:34,760
 description of the given boundary shape, this is one set of features that I can have.

143
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:41,400
 Another set of features that I can have is you see that when I represented this as a

144
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:51,920
 polygon I have missed a number of detailed information, so if I want to include some

145
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:56,920
 such detailed information in the description I generate, then I can follow it something

146
00:12:56,920 --> 00:13:03,040
 like this say given another boundary close boundary suppose I have a polygonal description

147
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:04,040
 like this.

148
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:09,880
 Out of this if I take just this particular segment on the left side, so segment is over

149
00:13:09,880 --> 00:13:16,400
 here, now the kind of processing that I can do over this segment is I represented this

150
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:22,760
 you just rotate this by an angle, so that the linear segments becomes horizontal and

151
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:29,720
 the curved boundary that particular segment now is a function of the distance on the linear

152
00:13:29,720 --> 00:13:34,400
 segment which was the linear approximation of this curve boundary.

153
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:39,840
 And if I normalize this particular segment in the sense the area between the curved segment

154
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:46,280
 and the horizontal axis I make it equal to 1, so that is the kind of normalization that

155
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:47,760
 I perform.

156
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:54,180
 So if the area between the curved segment and the horizontal axis becomes 1 then I can

157
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:57,800
 consider this to be a probability density function.

158
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:06,600
 So what I get is PR versus R that is the probability of occurrence of R, once I represent this

159
00:14:06,600 --> 00:14:14,000
 as a probability distribution function then I can compute different statistical moments.

160
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,680
 So you find that the statistical moment of order 2 this is very very familiar it is nothing

161
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:21,700
 but what is called variance.

162
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:27,360
 I can have other form of statistical variance statistical moment also, so statistical moment

163
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:32,600
 of order 3 which is called skewness, statistical moment of order 4 which is called kurtosis

164
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:39,320
 and all that, out of this the third order statistical moment which is skewness it is

165
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:45,600
 very easy to understand what it gives it gives whether your distribution is skewed towards

166
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:52,160
 left or skewed towards right, similarly each of these statistical moments give you some

167
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:55,960
 information of shape of the boundary.

168
00:14:55,960 --> 00:15:04,400
 So what I can do is earlier we have got the auto regression coefficients and I can concatenate

169
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:09,360
 with the set of auto regression coefficients all these statistical moment values.

170
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:12,360
 So I get a larger feature vector.

171
00:15:12,360 --> 00:15:17,600
 So this is the set of feature vector that you can get simply from the boundary information.

172
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:22,400
 Now what are the different types of region descriptors I can have?

173
00:15:22,400 --> 00:15:28,400
 You find again on the left I have shown the black and white image for that I can have intensity

174
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:33,960
 histogram I believe all of you know what is the intensity histogram and this intensity

175
00:15:33,960 --> 00:15:41,960
 histogram if I normalize that is sum of contents of all the histogram means is equal to 1 then

176
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:49,720
 this normalized intensity histogram can be considered as a frequency of occurrence of

177
00:15:49,720 --> 00:15:56,800
 different intensity values that means what is the probability of occurrence of different

178
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:58,520
 intensity value within this image.

179
00:15:58,520 --> 00:16:01,640
 So again it becomes a probability density function.

180
00:16:01,640 --> 00:16:06,600
 So once it is a probability density function again I can compute a different types of statistical

181
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:13,080
 moments which gives you information of what is the shape of the histogram of that black

182
00:16:13,080 --> 00:16:15,360
 and white image.

183
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:22,000
 Similarly if I want to find out the color distribution or color properties you know

184
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:27,800
 color image consists of three different planes the red, green and blue plane.

185
00:16:27,800 --> 00:16:34,040
 For each of the planes I can compute similar such histograms for each of the histograms

186
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:40,640
 you can compute the statistical moments all these things taken together gives you some

187
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:47,080
 information of how the intensity profile of the images, how the color profile of the images.

188
00:16:47,080 --> 00:16:54,080
 The same thing comes when it comes to this texture again from the texture I can compute

189
00:16:54,080 --> 00:17:00,440
 a different types of properties one of them is what is called a co-occurrence matrix.

190
00:17:00,440 --> 00:17:11,120
 The co-occurrence matrix is given two intensity values i and j and some positional constant

191
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:17,800
 say distance in this particular case I have shown a 45 degree ij that means if I take

192
00:17:17,800 --> 00:17:25,400
 two pixels two points p and q which are at a distance of one and oriented in the direction

193
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:37,000
 of 45 degree then if the intensity of pixel p is i let us let us say 10 and intensity

194
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:44,880
 at pixel say 15 then I want to find out that how many times these two intensity values

195
00:17:44,880 --> 00:17:54,040
 10 and 15 following this positional constant 145 degree occurs within that particular texture

196
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:57,440
 and that I do for every pair of intensity values.

197
00:17:57,440 --> 00:18:07,480
 So you find that for a particular positional constant say 145 degree I get one matrix which

198
00:18:07,480 --> 00:18:13,200
 is called a co-occurrence matrix that how many times 10 and 15 these two intensities

199
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:19,840
 following this particular constant occurred within this given image.

200
00:18:19,840 --> 00:18:24,120
 So this is for one constant positional constant I can compute this for multiple positional

201
00:18:24,120 --> 00:18:25,240
 constant.

202
00:18:25,240 --> 00:18:30,960
 So this co-occurrence matrix in that case will be different for different types of textures

203
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:36,080
 and once I have this co-occurrence matrix if I normalize this co-occurrence matrix that

204
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:43,800
 is some of all the elements of in the matrix I want to make it equal to one then it becomes

205
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:49,960
 a joint probability distribution function and the moment it becomes a joint probability

206
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:54,240
 distribution function again I compute all these I can compute all these different values

207
00:18:54,240 --> 00:19:00,280
 which gives you some information of what is the texture that I have and these values will

208
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:03,280
 be texture dependent.

209
00:19:03,280 --> 00:19:10,680
 So you find that we have computed descriptors based on the boundary and we have computed

210
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:15,440
 descriptors based on the intensity profile we have computed descriptors based on the

211
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:20,800
 color profile we have computed descriptors based on the texture.

212
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:26,160
 All these different quantities that we have computed if we concatenate them one after

213
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:31,600
 another then what we get is a feature vector and these feature vectors are likely to be

214
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:36,040
 different for different objects because different objects will have different shapes they will

215
00:19:36,040 --> 00:19:39,320
 have different colors they will have different intensity profile they will have different

216
00:19:39,320 --> 00:19:41,560
 color profile.

217
00:19:41,560 --> 00:19:45,160
 So once I have these vectors as I said that in my feature space if I have this hundreds

218
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:51,520
 such vectors hundreds such components feature components then I define a feature space of

219
00:19:51,520 --> 00:19:57,240
 dimension 100 and each of these vectors becomes a single point in that hundred dimensional

220
00:19:57,240 --> 00:19:58,600
 feature space.

221
00:19:58,600 --> 00:20:03,680
 So given two such vectors if the distance between these two vectors these two points

